"THE GENERALIZED BORWEIN CONJECTURE. I. THE BURGE TRANSFORM (https://people.smp.uq.edu.au/OleWarnaar/pubs/Borwein.pdf)\nciteturn4search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 15.5 years ago; Each identity in this Burge tree settles a special case of Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture. ... As so often in mathematics, the simplicity of the above claim is rather deceptive, and the conjecture still lacks proof.\nTHE GENERALIZED BORWEIN CONJECTURE. I. THE BURGE TRANSFORM\n\nS. OLE WARNAAR\n\n**Abstract.** Given an arbitrary ordered pair of coprime integers \\((a,b)\\) we obtain a pair of identities of the Rogers–Ramanujan type. These identities have the same product side as the (first) Andrews–Gordon identity for modulus \\(2ab \\pm 1\\), but an altogether different sum side, based on the representation of \\((a/b - 1)^{\\pm 1}\\) as a continued fraction. Our proof, which relies on the Burge transform, first establishes a binary tree of polynomial identities. Each identity in this Burge tree settles a special case of Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture.\n\n1. INTRODUCTION\n\nSeveral years ago P. Borwein communicated the following observation to G. E. Andrews [4].\n\n**Conjecture 1.1** (First Borwein conjecture). *The polynomials* \\(A_n(q)\\), \\(B_n(q)\\) *and* \\(C_n(q)\\) *defined by*\n\n\\[\n(1.1)\\qquad \\prod_{k=1}^{n}(1-q^{3k-2})(1-q^{3k-1})=A_n(q^3)-qB_n(q^3)-q^2C_n(q^3)\n\\]\n\n*have nonnegative coefficients.*\n\nAs so often in mathematics, the simplicity of the above claim is rather deceptive, and the conjecture still lacks proof. Following the motto ‘if you can’t prove it, generalize it’, Andrews [4], Bressoud [10], and Ismail, Kim and Stanton [14] have extended the Borwein conjecture in several directions. This is the first of a series of papers devoted to Bressoud’s generalization. To see how Bressoud’s conjecture arises most naturally from Conjecture 1.1 we follow Andrews [4] and rewrite \\(A_n\\), \\(B_n\\) and \\(C_n\\) as a sum over \\(q\\)-binomial coefficients. First we need the usual definitions of the \\(q\\)-shifted factorial\n\n\\[\n(a;q)_n=(a)_n=\\prod_{k=0}^{n-1}(1-aq^k)\\qquad \\text{for } n\\in \\mathbb{Z}_+\n\\]\n\nand \\(q\\)-binomial coefficient\n\n\\[\n(1.2)\\qquad \\begin{bmatrix} n \\\\ m \\end{bmatrix}_q=\\begin{bmatrix} n \\\\ m \\end{bmatrix}=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\dfrac{(q)_n}{(q)_m(q)_{n-m}} & \\text{for } m,n-m\\in \\mathbb{Z}_+ \\\\\n0 & \\text{otherwise.}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary 05A15, 05A19; Secondary 33D15.  \nWork supported by the Australian Research Council.\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAn analytic proof of the Borwein Conjecture - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0001870821004679)\nciteturn4search0 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.8 years ago; Crawled: last month; # An analytic proof of the Borwein Conjecture☆ ... ## Bressoud [8, Conjecture 6] ... It remains to see whether these ideas may also finally lead to a full proof of Bressoud's Conjecture.\n\n## Advances in Mathematics\n\nDate: 22 January 2022\n\nArticle: 108028\n\nVolume: Volume 394\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nOpen archive\n\nUnder an Elsevier user license\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# An analytic proof of the Borwein Conjecture☆\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Chen Wang\n\nShow more\n\nView PDFDownload full issue\n\n10.1016/j.aim.2021.108028\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe provide a proof of the Borwein Conjecture, which states that the coefficients of have a repeating sign pattern of , using analytic methods. The proof is done by utilizing an expansion by Andrews to extract the “main part” of the coefficients, and then bound the various “error terms” that arise from this expansion.\n\n## Keywords\n\nBorwein Conjecture\n\n;\n\nq-series\n\n;\n\nSaddle point method\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nIn 1990, Peter Borwein observed that for an arbitrary non-negative integer n, the coefficients of the polynomial have a repeating sign pattern of . \nConjecture 14.1\n\n## P. Borwein\n\nLet the polynomials , and be defined by the relationship(14.1) Then these polynomials have non-negative coefficients.\n\nConjecture 14.2\n\n## P. Borwein\n\nLet the polynomials , , , and be defined by the relationship(14.2) Then these polynomials have non-negative coefficients.\n\nBoth these conjectures are still wide open. In particular, no reasonable formulas for the polynomials have been found so far. We remark that the comparison of (1.1) and (14.1) yields the relationship , so non-negativity for the coefficients of follows trivially from this paper.\n\nRecall that for our proof we used the formulas for , and given in Theorem 1.2. As we mentioned, these formulas had apparently not caught much attention so far. It is rather a different type of formula that was found to be much more inspiring, namely (see [1, Theorem 3.1])(14.3){A}_{n}(q)=\\sum _{j\\in \\mathbb{Z}}{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{j(9j+1)/2}{\\left[\\begin{array}{c}2n\\\\ n+3j\\end{array}\\right]}_{q},(14.4){B}_{n}(q)=\\sum _{j\\in \\mathbb{Z}}{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{j(9j-5)/2}{\\left[\\begin{array}{c}2n\\\\ n+3j-1\\end{array}\\right]}_{q},(14.5){C}_{n}(q)=\\sum _{j\\in \\mathbb{Z}}{(-1)}^{j}{q}^{j(9j+7)/2}{\\left[\\begin{array}{c}2n\\\\ n+3j+1\\end{array}\\right]}_{q}, where we used again the standard notation for q-binomial coefficients. These are so much more imaginative because of their resemblance with a family of formulas appearing as generating functions for partitions with restricted hook differences in [2]. Andrews et al. had shown that(14.6) is the generating function for certain partitions with restricted hook differences, with being non-negative integers satisfying and . Indeed, the generating function in (14.3) is the “special case” of (14.6) in which , , and . Similar observations hold for and . In other words, the result of Andrews et al. seems to produce a proof of the Borwein Conjecture, except for the small flaw that the choices of α and β are not integral, and thus not legitimate.\n\nBressoud [8] extended the mystery by making the following much more general conjecture.\n\nConjecture 14.3\n\n## Bressoud [8, Conjecture 6]\n\nSuppose that , α and β are positive rational numbers, and K is a positive integer such that αK and βK are integers. If (with strict inequalities if ) and , then the polynomial has non-negative coefficients.\n\nTo this day, Bressoud's conjecture has only been proved when (corresponding to the result of Andrews et al. [2] mentioned above), and several infinite families of fractional parameters (see [3], [5], [7], [13], [18], [19]). The connection to partitions with hook difference conditions lets one hope that a similar combinatorial interpretation may exist for the polynomials in the Borwein Conjecture, but to this day no such connection has been found.\n\nOur approach for proving Theorem 12.1 has been analytic. The formulas that we just discussed, in particular the formulas (14.3)–(14.5) for , and , are unsuitable for asymptotic approximation. The reason is that each dominating term in the sums (14.3)–(14.5) has order , whereas the actual order of magnitude of , and is trivially bounded above by . --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDifference sets in ℝd - ORA - Oxford University Research Archive (https://ora.ox.ac.uk/objects/uuid%3Aecbd5145-9fc2-4c75-b250-fff2c6c44285)\nciteturn4search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.8 years ago; Crawled: 3 weeks ago; By a construction of Stanchescu, this is best possible and thus resolves an old question first raised by Uhrin. ...   * Conlon_and_Lim_2025_Difference_sets_in.pdf\n\nImage: Journal article icon\n\n# Journal article\n\n## Difference sets in ℝd\n\nAbstract:\n    Let d ≥ 2 be a natural number. We show that (Formula presented.) for any sufficiently large finite subset A of ℝd that is not contained in a translate of a hyperplane. By a construction of Stanchescu, this is best possible and thus resolves an old question first raised by Uhrin.\n\nPublication status:\n    Published\n\nPeer review status:\n    Peer reviewed\n\n\n  * Cite\n\n# Cite this record\n\n## APA Style\n\nConlon, D., & Lim, J. (2025). Difference sets in ℝd. Israel Journal of Mathematics, 267(2), 729–748.\n\nCopy APA Style [Input]\n\n## MLA Style\n\nConlon, D, and J Lim. “Difference Sets in ℝd.” Israel Journal of Mathematics, vol. 267, no. 2, 2025, pp. 729–48.\n\nCopy MLA Style [Input]\n\n## Chicago Style\n\nConlon, D, and J Lim. 2025. “Difference Sets in ℝd.” Israel Journal of Mathematics 267 (2): 729–48.\n\nCopy Chicago Style [Input]\n\n### Access Document\n\n* * *\n\nFiles:\n    \n\n  * Conlon_and_Lim_2025_Difference_sets_in.pdf\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDiﬀerence sets in Rd (https://www.its.caltech.edu/~dconlon/diffsets.pdf)\nciteturn4search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 6 months ago; common diﬀerence, was proven by Freiman, Heppes and Uhrin [3]. ... Supplanting an earlier conjecture of Ruzsa [8], Stanchescu proposed that this is best possible. ... However, the situation was considerably improved by Mudgal [6], ... Theorem 1.2.\nis the standard basis for Rd, and, for any natural number k, let Ak = (T ∪(ak −T)) + Pk, where\nak = ed−ked−1 and Pk = {e0, ed−1, 2ed−1, . . . , (k−1)ed−1}. Worked out carefully, this construction\nsatisﬁes\n\u0013\n|Ak| −(2d2 −4d + 3).\n|Ak −Ak| =\n\u0012\n2d −2 +\n1\nd −1\nSupplanting an earlier conjecture of Ruzsa [8], Stanchescu proposed that this is best possible.\nConjecture 1.1 (Stanchescu [10]). Suppose d ≥2 and A ⊂Rd is a ﬁnite set such that dim(A) = d.\nThen\n\u0013\n|A| −(2d2 −4d + 3).\n|A −A| ≥\n\u0012\n2d −2 +\n1\nd −1\nUntil very recently, little was known about this conjecture for d ≥4 besides the result of\nFreiman, Heppes and Uhrin [3]. However, the situation was considerably improved by Mudgal [6],\nwho showed that\n|A −A| ≥(2d −2)|A| −o(|A|)\nfor any ﬁnite d-dimensional subset A of Rd. Our main result, which builds on both Mudgal’s work\nand earlier work of Stanchescu [9, 12], is a proof of Conjecture 1.1 in full provided only that |A| is\nsuﬃciently large in terms of d, essentially resolving the problem of minimising the value of |A −A|\nover all d-dimensional sets A of a given size.\nTheorem 1.2. Suppose d ≥2 and A ⊂Rd is a ﬁnite set such that dim(A) = d. Then, provided\n|A| is suﬃciently large in terms of d,\n\u0013\n|A| −(2d2 −4d + 3).\n|A −A| ≥\n\u0012\n2d −2 +\n1\nd −1\nWe begin our proof of Theorem 1.2 in the next section with a result that we believe to be of\nindependent interest, an extension of a result of Stanchescu [12] about the structure of d-dimensional\nsubsets A of Rd with doubling constant smaller than d + 4/3 to asymmetric sums A + B.\n2--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nTHE GENERALIZED BORWEIN CONJECTURE. II. REFINED (https://people.smp.uq.edu.au/OleWarnaar/pubs/Borwein2.pdf)\nciteturn4search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7.3 years ago; The iterative nature of these transformations allows for the easy derivation of several infinite series of *q*-trinomial identities, and can be applied to prove many instances of Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture.\nTHE GENERALIZED BORWEIN CONJECTURE. II. REFINED  \n*q*-TRINOMIAL COEFFICIENTS\n\nS. OLE WARNAAR\n\n**Abstract.** Transformation formulas for four-parameter refinements of the *q*-trinomial coefficients are proven. The iterative nature of these transformations allows for the easy derivation of several infinite series of *q*-trinomial identities, and can be applied to prove many instances of Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture.\n\n## 1. Introduction\n\nThis is the second in a series of papers addressing Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture. Defining the Gaussian polynomial or *q*-binomial coefficient as\n\n(1.1)  \n\\[\n\\begin{bmatrix} m+n \\\\ m \\end{bmatrix}\n=\n\\begin{cases}\n\\prod_{k=1}^{m}\\frac{1-q^{n+k}}{1-q^k} & m,n\\in \\mathbb{Z}_+ \\\\\n0 & \\text{otherwise,}\n\\end{cases}\n\\]\n\n(with \\(\\mathbb{Z}_+ = \\{0,1,2,\\ldots\\}\\)) Bressoud [11] considered the polynomials\n\n\\[\nG(N,M;\\alpha,\\beta,K)=\\sum_{j=-\\infty}^{\\infty}(-1)^j q^{Kj((\\alpha+\\beta)j+\\alpha-\\beta)/2}\n\\begin{bmatrix} M+N \\\\ N-Kj \\end{bmatrix}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(P \\ge 0\\) if \\(P\\) is a polynomial with nonnegative coefficients, he then conjectured the truth of the following statement concerning \\(G\\).\n\n**Conjecture 1.1.** *Let \\(K\\) be a positive integer and \\(N,M,\\alpha K,\\beta K\\) be nonnegative integers such that \\(1 \\le \\alpha+\\beta \\le 2K-1\\) (strict inequalities when \\(K=2\\)) and \\(\\beta-K \\le N-M \\le K-\\alpha\\). Then \\(G(N,M;\\alpha,\\beta,K)\\ge 0\\).*\n\nThis generalizes an earlier conjecture of P. Borwein [5] stating that the coefficients of the polynomials \\(A_n(q)\\), \\(B_n(q)\\) and \\(C_n(q)\\), defined by\n\n\\[\n\\prod_{k=1}^{n}(1-q^{3k-2})(1-q^{3k-1})=A_n(q^3)-qB_n(q^3)-q^2C_n(q^3)\n\\]\n\nare all nonnegative. By the *q*-binomial theorem it readily follows that [5]\n\n\\[\nA_n(q)=G(n,n;4/3,5/3,3)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nB_n(q)=G(n+1,n-1;2/3,7/3,3)\n\\]\n\n\\[\nC_n(q)=G(n+1,n-1;1/3,8/3,3).\n\\]\n\n2000 Mathematics Subject Classification. Primary 05A15, 05A19; Secondary 33D15.  \nKey words and phrases. Gaussian polynomials, *q*-trinomial coefficients, Rogers–Ramanujan identities, basic hypergeometric series.  \nWork supported by the Australian Research Council.\n\n1--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPOSITIVITY PRESERVING TRANSFORMATIONS FOR (https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=5bc47efa29dbd7cfefd31d81aa341d159e090b56&repid=rep1&type=pdf)\nciteturn4search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 8 months ago; The Borwein conjecture and Bressoud’s generalization. ... Then the N = M case of Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture\n(3.34). After some simpliﬁcations this yields the claimed right-hand side.\n□\n9. The generalized Borwein conjecture\n9.1. The Borwein conjecture and Bressoud’s generalization. Some years\nago Peter Borwein conjectured [8] that the polynomials An(q), Bn(q) and Cn(q)\ngiven by\nAn(q3) −qBn(q3) −q2Cn(q3) = (q, q2; q3)n\nhave nonnegative coeﬃcients. Deﬁning\nG(N, M; α, β, K; q) = G(N, M; α, β, K)\n\u0015\n∞\nX\n=\nj=−∞\n(−1)jqKj((α+β)j+α−β)/2\n\u0014 M + N\nN −Kj\nit follows from (7.7) that [8]\nAn(q) = G(n, n; 4/3, 5/3, 3)\nBn(q) = G(n + 1, n −1; 2/3, 7/3, 3)\nCn(q) = G(n + 1, n −1; 1/3, 8/3, 3).\nThis led Bressoud [19] to a more general conjecture concerning the nonnegativity\nof the coeﬃcients of G(N, M; α, β, K; q). Since we will only be concerned with the\ncase N = M we write G(N; α, β, K; q) instead of G(N, N; α, β, K; q), in accordance\nwith (7.9). We also write P(q) ≥0 for P(q) a polynomial in q with nonnegative\ncoeﬃcients. Then the N = M case of Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture\ncan be stated as follows.\nConjecture 9.1. Let K, L, αK, βK be integers such that 0 ≤α ≤K, 0 ≤β ≤K\nand 1 ≤α + β ≤2K −1. Then G(L; α, β, K; q) ≥0.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBressoud's identities for even moduli. New companions and related positivity results - ScienceDirect (https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0012365X22003107)\nciteturn4search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: 3.9 years ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; We revisit Bressoud's generalized Borwein conjecture. ... In 1980, Bressoud [6] discovered even moduli identities:$$\\sum _{{n}_{1},{n}_{2},\\dots ,{n}_{v-1}\\ge 0}\\frac{{q}^{{N}_{1}^{2}+\\cdots +{N}_{v-1}^{2}+{N}_{i}+\\cdots +{N}_{v-1}}}{{(q)}_{{n}_{1}}{(q)}_{{n}_{2}}\\cdots {(q)}_{{n}_{v-2}}{({q}^{2};{q}^{2})}_{{n}_{v-1}}}=\\frac{{({q}^{2v},{q}^{i},{q}^{2v-i};{q}^{2v})}_{\\infty }}{{(q)}_{\\infty }}$$ with $v\\ge 2$, $1\\le i\\le v$, $|q|<1$ and ${N}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=i}^{v-1}{n}_{j}$.\n\n## Discrete Mathematics\n\nDate: December 2022\n\nArticle: 113104\n\nVolume: Volume 345, Issue 12\n\nView accessibility information\n\n## Published by: Elsevier\n\nResearch article\n\nGet rights and content\n\n# Bressoud's identities for even moduli. New companions and related positivity results\n\nTo the Memory of Omar Foda\n\nAuthor links open overlay panel Alexander Berkovich\n\nShow more\n\nCite\n\nAdd to Mendeley\n\nShare\n\n10.1016/j.disc.2022.113104\n\n  * Article preview\n  * Recommended articles\n  * Cited by (3)\n\n## Abstract\n\nWe revisit Bressoud's generalized Borwein conjecture. Making use of certain positivity-preserving transformations for q-binomial coefficients, we establish the truth of infinitely many new cases of the Bressoud conjecture. In addition, we prove new doubly-bounded refinement of the Foda-Quano identities. Finally, we discuss new companions to the Bressoud even moduli identities. In particular, all $10\\phantom{\\rule{0.50em}{0ex}}\\mathrm{mod}\\phantom{\\rule{0.25em}{0ex}}20$ identities are derived.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nThe hypergraph–QLR cone equality conjecture | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/345506/the-hypergraph-qlr-cone-equality-conjecture)\nciteturn4search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 weeks ago; # The hypergraph–QLR cone equality conjecture ... For each $n$n, let the $n$n-party hypergraph cone be the cone of entropy vectors obtained from $n$n-party hypergraphs, and let the $n$n-party QLR cone be the cone defined by the quantum linear rank inequalities. ... Su, “The Quantum Entropy Cone of Hypergraphs”, arXiv:2002.05317 (2020).\n\n# The hypergraph–QLR cone equality conjecture\n\nAbout 6 years old · traced to 2020\n\nFor each $n$n, let the $n$n-party hypergraph cone be the cone of entropy vectors obtained from $n$n-party hypergraphs, and let the $n$n-party QLR cone be the cone defined by the quantum linear rank inequalities. Hypergraph–QLR cone conjecture. The $n$n-party hypergraph and QLR cones coincide for all $n$n. The cones are known to coincide through four parties, and the paper gives contraction-map evidence for five parties; equality in general would identify the hypergraph cone with a cone of quantum-compatible entropy vectors.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nNing Bao, Newton Cheng, Sergio Hernández-Cuenca and Vincent P. Su, “The Quantum Entropy Cone of Hypergraphs”, arXiv:2002.05317 (2020).\n\nentropy-coneshypergraphsquantum-information\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPOSITIVITY PRESERVING TRANSFORMATIONS 3 (https://people.smp.uq.edu.au/OleWarnaar/pubs/Positivity.pdf)\nciteturn4search16 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 months ago; The reader may indeed have recognized (1.1) and (1.2) as special cases of the ordinary Bailey lemma in its version due to Andrews [6] and Paule [41], and (1.6)–(1.8) as special cases of base-changing extensions of the Bailey lemma discovered by Bressoud, Ismail and Stanton [20]. ... Finally, in Section 9, we use the positivity preserving nature of our results to make some progress on Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture.\nIn Section 6 this is exploited to yield some new (and old) transformations for basic hypergeometric series.\n\nThe Sections 7 and 8 deal with simple applications of the \\(q\\)-binomial transformations of section 2. In Section 7 new single and multisum identities of the Rogers–Ramanujan identities are proved and in Section 8 we obtain a remarkable new representation of the Rogers–Szegő polynomials.\n\nFinally, in Section 9, we use the positivity preserving nature of our results to make some progress on Bressoud’s generalized Borwein conjecture. In the last section we also prove new triple-sum representation for the Borwein polynomials and use this to formulate a new conjecture that implies the original Borwein conjecture.\n\n**2. Positivity preserving \\(q\\)-binomial transformations**\n\nThe reason that none of the transformations of the previous section preserves positivity is not a very deep one. Setting \\(q = 1\\) in (1.9) yields\n\n\\[\n\\sum_{\\substack{r=0 \\\\ r \\equiv j \\, (\\mathrm{mod}\\, 2)}}^{L} f_{L,r}(1)\\binom{r}{\\frac{1}{2}(r-j)}=\\binom{L}{\\frac{1}{2}(L-j)},\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nwhich has the unique solution \\(f_{L,r}(1)=\\delta_{L,r}\\). Hence the only polynomial solution to (1.9) that preserves positivity is the less-than-exciting \\(f_{L,r}(q)=\\delta_{L,r}\\) for \\(k=1\\) and--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n(PDF) Extension of Bressoud's generalization of Borwein's conjecture and some exact results (https://www.researchgate.net/publication/378496568_Extension_of_Bressoud%27s_generalization_of_Borwein%27s_conjecture_and_some_exact_results)\nciteturn4search4 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2.7 years ago; Crawled: 2 months ago; In this paper, we conjecture an extension to Bressoud's 1996 generalization of Borwein's famous 1990 conjecture. We then state two infinite hierarchies of non-negative q-series identities which are interesting and elegant examples of our proposed conjecture and Bressoud's generalized conjecture respectively.\n\n# Extension of Bressoud's generalization of Borwein's conjecture and some exact results\n\n  * February 2024\n\nAlexander Berkovich\n\n  * University of Florida\n\nAritram Dhar\n\n  * University of Florida\n\n\n## Abstract\n\nIn this paper, we conjecture an extension to Bressoud's 1996 generalization of Borwein's famous 1990 conjecture. We then state two infinite hierarchies of non-negative q-series identities which are interesting and elegant examples of our proposed conjecture and Bressoud's generalized conjecture respectively. F